WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.800
A water flea is transparent.

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.800
Put an infected one under a microscope, and you can watch its blood cells swallow

3
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.380
invading yeast spores.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.680
Where the cells destroyed the spores, the infection stopped.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.120
Where spores grew into budding yeast, the flea died.

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:28.253
Innate defenses are ready before an organism meets a particular microbe.

7
00:00:28.853 --> 00:00:33.793
In mammalian airways, mucus traps microbes, and cilia carry it out.

8
00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:39.413
A microbe that gets through meets a phagocyte, a cell that engulfs particles.

9
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:43.093
The microbe sits in a bubble, the phagosome.

10
00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:48.813
Lysosomes, sacs of digestive enzymes, fuse with it and kill the microbe.

11
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:53.827
In mammals, complement is a set of proteins in blood plasma.

12
00:00:54.687 --> 00:01:01.887
When C3 is cut, its large piece, C3b, sticks to a microbe and marks it for eating.

13
00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:08.527
The small pieces, C3a and C5a, drift away and promote inflammation.

14
00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:12.647
Later pieces can punch holes in susceptible microbes.

15
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:19.227
Interferons from virus-infected cells put nearby cells into an antiviral state.

16
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.407
Natural killer cells kill infected or abnormal cells.

17
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.820
In mammals, inflammation gathers these defenses.

18
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.820
Resident cells such as macrophages sense microbes with pattern-recognition

19
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:40.500
receptors. Mediators such as histamine widen small vessels and loosen their lining.

20
00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.220
Fluid and plasma proteins, complement among them, leak into the tissue.

21
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.020
Neutrophils squeeze out of the vessel, and monocytes follow.

22
00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.700
Macrophages then clear microbes and debris, and the tissue rebuilds.

23
00:01:57.433 --> 00:02:01.413
These numbers are illustrative, not a measured infection.

24
00:02:02.553 --> 00:02:07.853
Suppose 100 bacteria enter a wound, and each doubles every 20 minutes.

25
00:02:08.713 --> 00:02:16.393
With no defense, 4 hours is 12 doublings: 409,600 bacteria.

26
00:02:16.913 --> 00:02:24.373
If defenses kill 75% each interval, the multiplier is 0.5, and the infection is

27
00:02:24.373 --> 00:02:29.593
cleared. If they kill only 40%, the multiplier is 1.2.

28
00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:34.073
About 892 bacteria remain, still growing.

29
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:38.517
Assume constant rates and a well-mixed wound.

30
00:02:39.357 --> 00:02:46.157
Then the population shrinks only if 2 × (1 − fraction killed) is below 1.

31
00:02:47.057 --> 00:02:53.517
Keep one rule. In this model, a defense clears an infection only if it kills more

32
00:02:53.517 --> 00:02:56.297
than half the microbes in each doubling time.
